## Symptom
In a custom webpack 5 project an agent wires Tailwind v4 the v3 way (`postcss-loader` with `tailwindcss` in postcss.config), which fails with the "trying to use `tailwindcss` directly as a PostCSS plugin" error, or assumes PostCSS is the only webpack route.

## Cause
Tailwind CSS v4.2.0 (released 2026-02-18) added a first-party `@tailwindcss/webpack` package: a webpack loader that runs Tailwind directly, analogous to `@tailwindcss/vite`. Before 4.2 the only webpack route was `postcss-loader` + `@tailwindcss/postcss`, which still works.

## Fix
```bash
npm install tailwindcss @tailwindcss/webpack css-loader mini-css-extract-plugin
```
```js
// webpack.config.js
const MiniCssExtractPlugin = require('mini-css-extract-plugin')

module.exports = {
  plugins: [new MiniCssExtractPlugin()],
  module: {
    rules: [
      {
        test: /\.css$/i,
        use: [MiniCssExtractPlugin.loader, 'css-loader', '@tailwindcss/webpack'],
      },
    ],
  },
}
```
```css
/* src/index.css */
@import 'tailwindcss';
```
Loader options: `base` (directory to scan for class candidates, defaults to cwd) and `optimize` (minify, defaults to true in production). Remove `tailwindcss`/`autoprefixer` from any postcss.config if you switch.

## Verify
`npx webpack --mode development` emits a CSS file containing the utilities used in your sources.

## Sources
- https://github.com/tailwindlabs/tailwindcss/releases (v4.2.0: "Add @tailwindcss/webpack package to run Tailwind CSS as a webpack plugin (#19610)")
- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tailwindlabs/tailwindcss/main/packages/%40tailwindcss-webpack/README.md
- https://registry.npmjs.org/@tailwindcss/webpack/latest

## Symptom
After upgrading to Playwright 1.58+, selectors like `page.locator('_react=SubmitButton[enabled=true]')`, `_vue=...` or `css:light=...` fail because the engine no longer exists. `chromium.launch({ devtools: true })` no longer opens DevTools.

## Cause
Playwright 1.58 (2026-01-23) removed the experimental `_react` and `_vue` selector engines, the `:light` selector engine suffix, and the `devtools` option of `browserType.launch()`. It also dropped WebKit on macOS 13. Later releases removed more long-deprecated APIs. 1.63 (2026-09-04) removed the `videosPath` / `videoSize` context options (use `recordVideo`), the `logger` option on `connect` / `connectOverCDP`, and `Locator.ariaRef()`.

## Fix
```ts
// before
await page.locator('_react=SubmitButton[enabled=true]').click()
// after: user-facing locators, matched against the rendered DOM
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Submit' }).click()
await page.getByTestId('submit-button').click() // add data-testid in the component
```
```ts
// before
const browser = await chromium.launch({ devtools: true })
// after
const browser = await chromium.launch({ args: ['--auto-open-devtools-for-tabs'] })
```
```ts
// before (removed in 1.63)
const context = await browser.newContext({ videosPath: 'videos/' })
// after
const context = await browser.newContext({ recordVideo: { dir: 'videos/' } })
```
Replace `css:light=` with standard CSS selectors.

## Verify
`grep -rnE "_react=|_vue=|:light=|devtools: true|videosPath" tests` returns nothing, and the suite passes.

## Sources
- https://playwright.dev/docs/release-notes

## Symptom
After upgrading to React 19 (or installing a fresh project that pulls React 19, e.g. Next.js 15+), the app or a dependency crashes at mount with `TypeError: react_dom__WEBPACK_IMPORTED_MODULE_0__.render is not a function` (or `ReactDOM.render is not a function` with a CDN/ESM build). The same applies to `ReactDOM.hydrate` and `unmountComponentAtNode`.

## Cause
`ReactDOM.render`, `ReactDOM.hydrate`, `unmountComponentAtNode` and `findDOMNode` were deprecated in React 18 and **removed** in React 19 (stable 2024-12-05). Code examples from older tutorials and older libraries (e.g. leva < 0.10.0) still call them.

## Fix
Your own code — use the root API from `react-dom/client`:
```js
// Before
import {render, hydrate, unmountComponentAtNode} from 'react-dom';
render(<App />, document.getElementById('root'));
hydrate(<App />, document.getElementById('root'));
unmountComponentAtNode(document.getElementById('root'));

// After
import {createRoot, hydrateRoot} from 'react-dom/client';
const root = createRoot(document.getElementById('root'));
root.render(<App />);
// SSR: hydrateRoot(document.getElementById('root'), <App />);
root.unmount();
```
Codemod: `npx codemod@latest react/19/replace-reactdom-render`.

`findDOMNode` → attach a `useRef` to the element and read `ref.current`.

If the call is inside a dependency (stack trace in `node_modules`), upgrade that library to its React-19-compatible release (leva fixed it in 0.10.0) or stay on React 18 until it does.

## Verify
`grep -rn "ReactDOM.render\|from 'react-dom'" src` returns no `render`/`hydrate`/`unmountComponentAtNode` imports; the app mounts without the TypeError.

## Sources
- https://react.dev/blog/2024/04/25/react-19-upgrade-guide
- https://github.com/pmndrs/leva/issues/521

## Symptom
With Content Layer collections (default in Astro 5, the only option in Astro 6), `post.slug` is `undefined`, so `getStaticPaths` produces broken routes. `await post.render()` throws because entries no longer have a `render` method. Models still write `params: { slug: post.slug }` and `post.render()`.

## Cause
Astro 5.0 (2024-12-03) introduced the Content Layer API. Its entries are serializable plain objects: `id` is now the slug, there is no `slug` property, and rendering is a standalone function. Astro 6.0 (2026-03-10) removed the legacy API that still provided `slug` and `entry.render()`. The old filename-based id is now available as `entry.filePath`. `getEntryBySlug()` and `getDataEntryById()` were also removed; use `getEntry()`.

## Fix
```astro
---
// src/pages/blog/[slug].astro
import { getCollection, render } from 'astro:content'

export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const posts = await getCollection('blog')
  return posts.map((post) => ({
    params: { slug: post.id },   // was: post.slug
    props: post,
  }))
}
const post = Astro.props
const { Content, headings } = await render(post)   // was: await post.render()
---
<Content />
```
```ts
// before
const entry = await getEntryBySlug('blog', slug)
// after
const entry = await getEntry('blog', slug)
```

## Verify
`npx astro build` lists one generated route per entry, with the expected URL segments.

## Sources
- https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/upgrade-to/v5/
- https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/upgrade-to/v6/

## Symptom
An agent tells the user to install `react@experimental` / `react@canary` to use `<ViewTransition>` or `addTransitionType` (as the April 2025 React Labs post instructed), or claims they don't exist in stable React. Conversely, on React <= 19.2 `import { ViewTransition } from 'react'` yields `undefined` (rendering it fails) because 19.2.0 stable does not export it.

## Cause
React 19.3.0 (2026-09-09) made `<ViewTransition>` and Fragment Refs stable, added `addTransitionType` and `browser()` (react-dom) and allows `<Context>` to be rendered directly in Server Components. Verified on npm: `react@19.2.0` exports `Activity` and `useEffectEvent` but not `ViewTransition`; `react@19.3.0` exports `ViewTransition`. No React 20 exists as of October 2026.

## Fix
```bash
npm i react@^19.3 react-dom@^19.3
```
```js
import { ViewTransition, startTransition, addTransitionType } from 'react';

{isShowing && (
  <ViewTransition>
    <Component />
  </ViewTransition>
)}

function nextSlide() {
  startTransition(() => {
    addTransitionType('next');
    setCurrentSlide((c) => c + 1);
  });
}
```
Opt a component out of SSR:
```js
import { use } from 'react';
import { browser } from 'react-dom';
function ClientOnly() { use(browser()); /* ... */ }
```
`<ViewTransition>` is DOM-only for now (not React Native). Frameworks pinning their own React (e.g. Next.js App Router) may need their own flag/version.

## Verify
`node -p "typeof require('react').ViewTransition"` prints `symbol` (not `undefined`) and `npm ls react` shows 19.3.x.

## Sources
- https://react.dev/blog/2026/09/09/react-19-3
- https://react.dev/blog/2025/04/23/react-labs-view-transitions-activity-and-more
- https://registry.npmjs.org/react

## Symptom
After upgrading to React 19, a function component's `Component.defaultProps = {...}` no longer applies (props come through `undefined`), and `Component.propTypes` validation warnings stop appearing.

## Cause
React 19 (stable 2024-12-05) removed `propTypes` checks entirely (they are silently ignored) and removed `defaultProps` for function components. `defaultProps` still works for class components only.

## Fix
```js
// Before
import PropTypes from 'prop-types';
function Heading({text}) { return <h1>{text}</h1>; }
Heading.propTypes = { text: PropTypes.string };
Heading.defaultProps = { text: 'Hello, world!' };
```
```ts
// After
interface Props { text?: string }
function Heading({text = 'Hello, world!'}: Props) {
  return <h1>{text}</h1>;
}
```
Codemod: `npx codemod@latest react/prop-types-typescript`.

## Verify
Render the component without the prop and confirm the default shows; `grep -rn "defaultProps" src` only matches class components.

## Sources
- https://react.dev/blog/2024/04/25/react-19-upgrade-guide

## Symptom
After upgrading to Jest 30, `jest.genMockFromModule('fs')` no longer exists (runtime TypeError, or a TS error that the property does not exist). Code that annotates spies as `jest.SpyInstance` with `import { jest } from '@jest/globals'` fails to type-check.

## Cause
Jest 30.0.0 (2025-06-04) removed the long-deprecated `jest.genMockFromModule()` (PR #15042). It also removed the public types `MockFunctionMetadata`, `MockFunctionMetadataType` and `SpyInstance` from the `@jest/globals` API. Jest 30 also requires TypeScript 5.4+ for its type definitions and Node 18+.

## Fix
```ts
// before (Jest 29)
const mockFs = jest.genMockFromModule('fs');
let spy: jest.SpyInstance;

// after (Jest 30)
const mockFs = jest.createMockFromModule('fs');
let spy: jest.Spied<typeof obj.method>;
```
The behaviour of `createMockFromModule` is the same as the old function.

## Verify
`grep -rn genMockFromModule .` returns nothing outside node_modules, and `tsc --noEmit` passes with TypeScript >= 5.4.

## Sources
- https://jestjs.io/docs/upgrading-to-jest30
- https://github.com/jestjs/jest/releases/tag/v30.0.0

## Symptom
An agent writes `import { useFormState } from 'react-dom'` (the Canary/Next 14 era API) in a React 19 / Next.js 15+ app and gets a deprecation warning, or reaches for `useFormStatus`/manual state for pending UI when the hook already provides it.

## Cause
In React 19 (stable 2024-12-05) `ReactDOM.useFormState` was renamed to `React.useActionState`, moved from `react-dom` to `react`, and deprecated under its old name (react PR #28491). The new hook also returns a third value, `isPending`. The action's signature is `(previousState, payload)` — the FormData is the **second** argument.

## Fix
```diff
- import { useFormState } from 'react-dom';
- const [state, formAction] = useFormState(action, initialState);
+ import { useActionState } from 'react';
+ const [state, formAction, isPending] = useActionState(action, initialState);
```
```js
async function action(previousState, formData) {
  const error = await updateName(formData.get('name'));
  return error ?? null;
}
// <form action={formAction}> ... <button disabled={isPending}>Save</button>
```
Codemod (in the React 19 recipe): `npx codemod@latest react/19/migration-recipe` (includes `replace-use-form-state`).

## Verify
No `useFormState` imports remain; the form shows pending state via `isPending` and no deprecation warning is logged.

## Sources
- https://react.dev/blog/2024/12/05/react-19
- https://react.dev/blog/2024/04/25/react-19-upgrade-guide

## Symptom
After upgrading to `@types/react@19`, references like `JSX.Element` or `JSX.IntrinsicElements` fail with "Cannot find namespace 'JSX'"-style errors, and a `global.d.ts` that adds custom elements via `declare global { namespace JSX { ... } }` / top-level `namespace JSX` stops taking effect.

## Cause
`@types/react` 19 (React 19 stable 2024-12-05) removed the global `JSX` namespace in favor of `React.JSX`, to avoid global-type conflicts between JSX libraries.

## Fix
Type references:
```diff
- function App(): JSX.Element {
+ function App(): React.JSX.Element {
```
(or `import type { JSX } from 'react'`).

Custom element augmentation — wrap it in a module declaration:
```diff
// global.d.ts
+ declare module "react" {
    namespace JSX {
      interface IntrinsicElements {
        "my-element": { myElementProps: string };
      }
    }
+ }
```
Module specifier by tsconfig `jsx`: `react-jsx` → `react/jsx-runtime`; `react-jsxdev` → `react/jsx-dev-runtime`; `react`/`preserve` → `react`.
Codemod: `npx types-react-codemod@latest scoped-jsx ./src`.

## Verify
`tsc --noEmit` passes and `<my-element myElementProps="x" />` type-checks.

## Sources
- https://react.dev/blog/2024/04/25/react-19-upgrade-guide

## Symptom
FFI code that compiled under edition 2021 fails under `edition = "2024"`:

```
error: extern blocks must be unsafe
error: unsafe attribute used without unsafe
```

The second points at `#[no_mangle]`, `#[export_name = ...]` or `#[link_section = ...]`.

## Cause
The 2024 edition (Rust 1.85.0, 2025-02-20) requires `unsafe` on `extern` blocks and wraps symbol/link attributes in `unsafe(...)`, because wrong signatures or symbol collisions cause undefined behaviour the compiler cannot check. The new syntax has been accepted in all editions since Rust 1.82, so you can migrate before switching editions.

## Fix
```rust
// before (2021)
extern "C" {
    fn strlen(p: *const std::ffi::c_char) -> usize;
}
#[no_mangle]
pub extern "C" fn my_fn() {}

// after (2024)
unsafe extern "C" {
    pub safe fn sqrt(x: f64) -> f64;                          // callable without unsafe
    pub unsafe fn strlen(p: *const std::ffi::c_char) -> usize;
}
#[unsafe(no_mangle)]
pub extern "C" fn my_fn() {}
```
Automated: `cargo fix --edition` (lints `missing_unsafe_on_extern`, `unsafe_attr_outside_unsafe`), then review the extern signatures by hand. Items in an `unsafe extern` block default to unsafe unless marked `safe`.

## Verify
`cargo build` succeeds with `edition = "2024"`; `grep -rn '#\[no_mangle\]' src` returns nothing.

## Sources
- https://doc.rust-lang.org/edition-guide/rust-2024/unsafe-extern.html
- https://doc.rust-lang.org/edition-guide/rust-2024/unsafe-attributes.html
- https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/master/compiler/rustc_ast_passes/src/diagnostics.rs

## Symptom
Code (often a library doing `React.cloneElement` / reading `child.ref`) logs `Accessing element.ref is no longer supported. ref is now a regular prop...`. Separately, agents still wrap every new component in `forwardRef` out of habit.

## Cause
React 19 (stable 2024-12-05) passes `ref` to function components as a normal prop. `element.ref` is deprecated (read `element.props.ref`). `forwardRef` is no longer needed for function components and React says it will be deprecated and removed in a future version. Refs passed to class components are still not props.

## Fix
```diff
- const MyInput = forwardRef(function MyInput({placeholder}, ref) {
-   return <input placeholder={placeholder} ref={ref} />;
- });
+ function MyInput({placeholder, ref}) {
+   return <input placeholder={placeholder} ref={ref} />;
+ }
```
TypeScript: type it as a prop, e.g. `{ ref?: React.Ref<HTMLInputElement> }` or `React.ComponentProps<'input'>`.

Reading another element's ref:
```diff
- const childRef = child.ref;
+ const childRef = child.props.ref;
```
If the warning comes from a dependency, upgrade it.

## Verify
`<MyInput ref={r} />` sets `r.current` to the input; the element.ref warning no longer appears.

## Sources
- https://react.dev/blog/2024/12/05/react-19
- https://react.dev/blog/2024/04/25/react-19-upgrade-guide

## Symptom
JS/TS code uses `import { GoogleGenerativeAI } from "@google/generative-ai"` and `genAI.getGenerativeModel({ model })`, the pattern in most 2024 Gemini tutorials.

## Cause
Google's Gen AI SDK replaced the legacy JS library; Gemini API docs only show the new SDK, and the migration guide says users of legacy libraries should migrate.

## Fix
```bash
npm uninstall @google/generative-ai && npm install @google/genai
```
```ts
// before
import { GoogleGenerativeAI } from "@google/generative-ai";
const genAI = new GoogleGenerativeAI(process.env.GEMINI_API_KEY);
// after
import { GoogleGenAI } from "@google/genai";
const ai = new GoogleGenAI({ apiKey: [REDACTED] });
const res = await ai.models.generateContent({ model: "gemini-3.8-flash", contents: "Hello" });
console.log(res.text);
```

## Verify
`npm ls @google/generative-ai` is empty.

## Sources
- https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/migrate

## Symptom
A web console wraps `claude -p`. Users send a correction while the agent is still working; with one process per message the correction has to wait for the whole turn, or the run must be killed.

## Fix
Keep one long-lived process per conversation with streaming JSON on both sides and write new user messages to its stdin:
```js
const p = spawn('claude', ['-p', '--input-format', 'stream-json', '--output-format', 'stream-json',
  '--verbose', '--resume', sessionId /* optional */], { stdio: ['pipe', 'pipe', 'pipe'] });
const send = text => p.stdin.write(JSON.stringify({
  type: 'user', message: { role: 'user', content: [{ type: 'text', text }] } }) + '\n');
send(firstAsk);
// later, while busy:
send('[follow-up] ' + correction);
// parse stdout line by line; a {"type":"result", ...} event marks the end of a turn
```
- A message written while a turn is running is picked up at the agent's next tool call.
- Treat the `result` event as turn end (release locks, run post-steps, dequeue).
- Idle-kill the process after N minutes; resume later with `--resume <session_id>`.
- Kill it explicitly on reset/stop so orphaned processes don't pile up.

## Verify
Test harness: a message sent 4 s into a run was acted on at 13 s. In production a steer sent 5.5 s into a run was acknowledged by the agent at 13.8 s in the same turn.

## Notes
Do not restart the parent server while a turn is running — the child dies with its stdio pipe. Track script children (build/deploy) separately from the agent process slot; overwriting the slot leaked ~8 idle agent processes (2.5 GB RSS) before it was fixed.

## Symptom
Users get logged out at random, or the console prints the warning above, or the variant: "...configured without set and remove cookie methods... Consider switching to the getAll and setAll cookie methods instead of get, set and remove which are deprecated and can be difficult to use correctly." Older code uses `@supabase/auth-helpers-nextjs` (`createServerComponentClient`, `createMiddlewareClient`) or `createServerClient` with `cookies: { get, set, remove }`.

## Cause
The `@supabase/auth-helpers-*` packages are deprecated and consolidated into `@supabase/ssr`. In `@supabase/ssr`, the `get`/`set`/`remove` cookie methods are marked `@deprecated` ("These will not be supported in the next major version"). @supabase/ssr 0.12.0 (2026-06-09) also republished the SSR code under the deprecated auth-helpers package names. Those packages print a "Package Consolidation Notice" telling you to install @supabase/ssr directly. The supported shape is `getAll` + `setAll`, and `setAll` has to be able to write refreshed tokens back to the response.

## Fix
```bash
npm uninstall @supabase/auth-helpers-nextjs && npm install @supabase/ssr
```
```ts
// lib/supabase/server.ts (Next.js 15/16 — cookies() is async)
import { createServerClient } from "@supabase/ssr"
import { cookies } from "next/headers"
export async function createClient() {
  const cookieStore = await cookies()
  return createServerClient(
    process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL!,
    process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY!,
    { cookies: {
        getAll() { return cookieStore.getAll() },
        setAll(cookiesToSet) {
          try { cookiesToSet.forEach(({ name, value, options }) => cookieStore.set(name, value, options)) }
          catch { /* called from a Server Component; proxy refreshes the session */ }
        },
    } },
  )
}
```
In proxy.ts (middleware.ts before Next 16), return the same `supabaseResponse` that `setAll` wrote to. If you build a new response, copy the cookies over: `myNewResponse.cookies.setAll(supabaseResponse.cookies.getAll())`.

## Verify
The warning stops, and sessions survive past access-token expiry (default 1h) without a forced re-login.

## Sources
- https://github.com/supabase/ssr/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md
- https://github.com/supabase/ssr/blob/main/src/createServerClient.ts
- https://github.com/supabase/ssr/blob/main/src/warnDeprecatedPackage.ts
- https://supabase.com/docs/guides/auth/server-side/creating-a-client
- https://supabase.com/docs/guides/auth/server-side/migrating-to-ssr-from-auth-helpers

## Symptom
Automating an already-logged-in Chrome via `chromium.connectOverCDP('http://127.0.0.1:9222')`: navigation and clicks work, but `page.screenshot()` never resolves.

## Fix
Take the screenshot through a raw CDP session:
```js
const { chromium } = require('playwright-core');
const browser = await chromium.connectOverCDP('http://127.0.0.1:9222');
const ctx = browser.contexts()[0];
const page = ctx.pages()[0] || await ctx.newPage();
const cdp = await ctx.newCDPSession(page);
const { data } = await cdp.send('Page.captureScreenshot', { format: 'png' });
require('fs').writeFileSync('shot.png', Buffer.from(data, 'base64'));
```
Elements inside dropdown/"More" menus that ignore `locator.click()` can be clicked with `await el.evaluate(e => e.click())`.

## Notes
To drive a logged-in profile without disturbing the user's running Chrome, copy the profile directory (skip `Cache`, `Code Cache`, `GPUCache`, `Service Worker`) plus `Local State` to a new user-data-dir, delete `Singleton*` lock files, and launch a second Chrome with `--user-data-dir=<copy> --profile-directory="<Profile N>" --remote-debugging-port=9222 --no-first-run`. Recent Chrome versions refuse remote debugging on the default user-data-dir, which is another reason to use a copy. On the same macOS user the cookies decrypt (Keychain), so the copy contains live sessions — delete it when done.

## Verify
Screenshots return immediately via CDP.

## Symptom
A web console that streamed `claude -p --output-format stream-json --verbose` showed the assistant's text and tool calls, but never the tool outputs (command output, file contents), so transcripts looked half-empty.

## Cause
The stream mirrors the API message structure. `tool_use` blocks appear in `{"type":"assistant"}` events, but the matching `tool_result` blocks come back in `{"type":"user"}` events (role user, content array with `tool_result` items carrying `tool_use_id`). A UI that only renders assistant events drops them.

## Fix
```js
for (const ev of lines.map(JSON.parse)) {
  if (ev.type === 'assistant') for (const b of ev.message.content) {
    if (b.type === 'text') ui.text(b.text);
    if (b.type === 'tool_use') ui.toolCall(b.id, b.name, b.input);
  }
  if (ev.type === 'user' && Array.isArray(ev.message.content)) for (const b of ev.message.content)
    if (b.type === 'tool_result') ui.toolResult(b.tool_use_id, b.content, b.is_error);
  if (ev.type === 'result') ui.done(ev.usage, ev.session_id);
}
```
Add `--include-partial-messages` to get token-level deltas for a live typing effect.

## Token accounting surprise
Cumulative input tokens look huge (hundreds of thousands for a 5-step turn) because each step re-sends the whole context, most of it cache reads billed at about 1/10. A resumed session cost about 115k input tokens for a trivial request, against about 20k fresh (the fixed floor is system prompt, tool definitions and CLAUDE.md). Offer a "new topic" reset that drops the resume id.

## Verify
After handling `user` events, the console showed full tool input and output pairs, including red/green diffs for Edit/Write.

## Symptom
Agent advice based on older Vercel limits (e.g. "Hobby functions time out after 10–60s, Pro defaults to well under 5 minutes — set `maxDuration` or move to the Edge runtime") is outdated. Separately, code that keeps per-request data in module-level variables leaks data between users or behaves nondeterministically on newer projects, because several invocations now run in the same process at once.

## Cause
- "As of April 23, 2025, fluid compute is enabled by default for new projects."
- 2025-06-25: Fluid defaults raised to **300s default / 300s max on Hobby** (up from 60s), **300s default (up from 90s) / 800s max on Pro and Enterprise** (30-minute extended max in beta for supported Node.js/Bun/Python runtimes, set per function).
- Fluid compute uses "optimized concurrency": multiple invocations share a single function instance (Node.js/Python), so global state is shared.

## Fix
Only set `maxDuration` when you need more than 300s (Pro/Enterprise) or less:

```ts
// app/api/report/route.ts (Next.js) — function code has the highest precedence
export const maxDuration = 800;
```

```json
// vercel.json — opt a project/deployment into Fluid explicitly
{ "$schema": "https://openapi.vercel.sh/vercel.json", "fluid": true }
```

Keep request-scoped data out of module scope:

```ts
// before — unsafe under concurrency
let currentUser: User | null = null;
export async function GET(req: Request) { currentUser = await auth(req); ... }
// after
export async function GET(req: Request) { const user = await auth(req); ... }
```

Use `waitUntil` (from `@vercel/functions`) for post-response work instead of fire-and-forget promises. For jobs beyond the max duration, use Vercel Workflows/queues rather than raising limits. Older projects created before 2025-04-23 must enable Fluid in Settings → Functions (or `"fluid": true`).

