- https://tailwindcss.com/blog/tailwindcss-v4-1
- https://tailwindcss.com/blog/tailwindcss-v4
- https://tailwindcss.com/docs/upgrade-guide#using-a-javascript-config-file

## Symptom
After upgrading to `@types/react@19`, `useRef()` and `createContext()` called with no arguments fail type-checking (the upgrade guide annotates them `Expected 1 argument but saw none`). Code that imports `MutableRefObject` gets deprecation hints.

## Cause
`@types/react` 19 (React 19 stable 2024-12-05) makes `useRef` require an argument, like `createContext`. At the same time all refs became mutable: `MutableRefObject` is deprecated in favor of a single `RefObject<T> { current: T }` that `useRef` always returns. `useRef<T>(null)` returns `RefObject<T | null>` and `useRef(undefined)` returns `RefObject<T | undefined>`. (The old React 18 annoyance `Cannot assign to 'current' because it is a read-only property` after `useRef<number>(null)` is gone.)

## Fix
```diff
- const ref = useRef();
+ const ref = useRef(undefined);
- const Ctx = createContext();
+ const Ctx = createContext(undefined);
- const r: React.MutableRefObject<HTMLDivElement | null> = useRef(null);
+ const r: React.RefObject<HTMLDivElement | null> = useRef(null);
```
Automated: `npx types-react-codemod@latest preset-19 ./src` (includes `refobject-defaults`).

## Verify
`tsc --noEmit` passes with `@types/react@^19`.

## Sources
- https://react.dev/blog/2024/04/25/react-19-upgrade-guide

## Symptom
Running a `.ts` file directly with `node file.ts` (no flags) fails with `SyntaxError [ERR_UNSUPPORTED_TYPESCRIPT_SYNTAX]: TypeScript enum is not supported in strip-only mode`. Same failure class for `namespace` with runtime code, constructor parameter properties (`constructor(private x: number)`), and `import x = require()` aliases; decorators give a parser error. It can also appear unexpectedly in CI/tests after a Node upgrade because Node now strips `.ts` before an older loader (ts-node, esbuild-register) gets a chance.

## Cause
Node's built-in TypeScript support only *erases* types. Type stripping became default (unflagged) in v23.6.0 / v22.18.0, stopped warning in v24.3.0, and was marked stable in v25.2.0 / v24.12.0. Syntax that needs code generation is rejected. The escape hatch `--experimental-transform-types` was **removed in Node 26.0.0 (2026-05-05)**, so on Node 26 there is no flag that makes enums work.

## Fix
Replace non-erasable syntax:
```ts
// Before
enum Colour { Red, Blue }

// After
export const Colour = { Red: 0, Blue: 1 } as const;
export type Colour = (typeof Colour)[keyof typeof Colour];
```
```ts
// Before
class P { constructor(private x: number) {} }
// After
class P { private x: number; constructor(x: number) { this.x = x; } }
```
Make `tsc` catch it up front (TS 5.8+), per the Node docs:
```json
{ "compilerOptions": { "target": "esnext", "module": "nodenext", "noEmit": true,
  "rewriteRelativeImportExtensions": true, "erasableSyntaxOnly": true, "verbatimModuleSyntax": true } }
```
If you must keep enums: compile with `tsc`, or run with `tsx`. On Node 22–25 only, `node --experimental-transform-types file.ts` still works.

## Verify
`npx tsc --noEmit` reports no TS1294 errors, and `node file.ts` runs.

## Sources
- https://nodejs.org/api/typescript.html
- https://nodejs.org/en/blog/release/v26.0.0
- https://github.com/TypeStrong/ts-node/issues/2175

## Symptom
After upgrading to Prisma 7, code like `prisma.$use(async (params, next) => { ... })` fails: `$use` does not exist on the client (TypeScript reports the property does not exist; at runtime the call throws a TypeError). Wrong assumption: `$use` middleware is still the way to hook every query (soft delete, logging, timing).

## Cause
Prisma ORM 7.0.0 (2025-11-19) removed client middleware (`$use`), which had been deprecated in favor of Client Extensions. The Metrics preview feature (deprecated in 6.14.0) was removed in the same release.

## Fix
Before:
```ts
prisma.$use(async (params, next) => {
  const start = Date.now()
  const result = await next(params)
  console.log(`${params.model}.${params.action} ${Date.now() - start}ms`)
  return result
})
```
After:
```ts
const prisma = new PrismaClient({ adapter }).$extends({
  query: {
    $allModels: {
      async $allOperations({ model, operation, args, query }) {
        const start = Date.now()
        const result = await query(args)
        console.log(`${model}.${operation} ${Date.now() - start}ms`)
        return result
      },
    },
  },
})
```
For a single model/operation use e.g. `query: { user: { async findMany({ args, query }) { ... return query(args) } } }`. Note `$extends` returns a new client — export and use the extended instance.

## Verify
No `$use(` left in the codebase; the hook fires on a test query.

## Sources
- https://www.prisma.io/docs/orm/more/upgrade-guides/upgrading-versions/upgrading-to-prisma-7
- https://github.com/prisma/prisma/releases/tag/7.0.0

## Symptom
Cleanup scripts that call `fs.rmdirSync(dir, { recursive: true })` / `fs.promises.rmdir(dir, { recursive: true })` stop removing non-empty directories on Node 25+ (they fail like a plain `rmdir` on a non-empty directory). Agents trained on older Node often still emit this pattern; on Node 16–24 it only printed DEP0147 warnings.

## Cause
DEP0147 moved to End-of-Life in Node 25.0.0 (2025-10-15, "fs: move rmdir recursive option to end-of-life", PR #58616): "The `fs.rmdir`, `fs.rmdirSync`, and `fs.promises.rmdir` methods used to support a `recursive` option. That option has been removed." Node 25 also removed `fs.F_OK`, `fs.R_OK`, `fs.W_OK`, `fs.X_OK` (DEP0176) — use `fs.constants.*`.

## Fix
```diff
- fs.rmdirSync(dir, { recursive: true });
+ fs.rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });
- await fs.promises.rmdir(dir, { recursive: true });
+ await fs.promises.rm(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });
- fs.accessSync(p, fs.R_OK);
+ fs.accessSync(p, fs.constants.R_OK);
```
(`force: true` makes a missing path a no-op, matching the old rimraf-style behaviour.)

## Verify
Run the script on Node 25+: the directory is gone and `grep -rn "rmdir.*recursive\|fs\.[FRWX]_OK" src` returns nothing.

## Sources
- https://nodejs.org/api/deprecations.html
- https://nodejs.org/en/blog/release/v25.0.0

## Goal
Record training/demo videos of a web app against a local copy with disposable data, while the address bar and any links show the real production hostname, without touching production or editing /etc/hosts.

## Recipe
```js
import { chromium } from "playwright";
const REAL = process.env.BASE || "http://localhost:3000";          // local server
const BASE = process.env.NOMAP ? REAL : "http://app.example.com";  // what the viewer sees
const MAPARG = `--host-resolver-rules=MAP app.example.com:80 127.0.0.1:${new URL(REAL).port}`;
const br = await chromium.launch({ channel: "chrome", headless: true, args: [MAPARG] });
const ctx = await br.newContext({ viewport: { width: 1440, height: 810 } });
await ctx.addCookies([{ name: "session", value: token, url: BASE }]);  // cookies for the displayed host
const pg = await ctx.newPage();
await pg.goto(BASE + "/");
```
Chromium resolves `app.example.com:80` to the local port, so every request, cookie and redirect stays local while the URL bar shows the real domain. Use plain `http://`. With https the local server would need a certificate for the production name.

## Gotcha
A log of `page.on("framenavigated")` printed `NAV http://app.example.com/changes` mid-recording and looked like the script had reached production. It hadn't: with the mapping active, all navigation shows the mapped host. To check, re-run with the mapping disabled (`NOMAP=1`, URL becomes `http://localhost:PORT/...`) or watch the local server's request log. Keep a kill switch like `NOMAP` in the script for that reason.

## Verify
The same script with `NOMAP=1` navigated `http://localhost:3000/` → `/changes`. With the mapping, it showed `http://app.example.com/...` while the local server's fresh test data appeared on screen and production data stayed unchanged.

## Symptom
After upgrading to v4, focus rings written as `focus:ring` become a thin 1px ring in the text color instead of the 3px blue ring, and every `border` / `divide-y` without a color class turns dark (text color) instead of light gray.

## Cause
Tailwind CSS v4.0 (2025-01-22) changed defaults for consistency with browser behaviour:
- `ring` width 3px → 1px, and default ring color `blue-500` → `currentColor`.
- `border-*` and `divide-*` default color: configured `gray-200` → `currentColor`.

## Fix
Be explicit:
```html
<!-- v3 -->
<button class="focus:ring">
<div class="border px-2 py-3">
<!-- v4 -->
<button class="focus:ring-3 focus:ring-blue-500">
<div class="border border-gray-200 px-2 py-3">
```
Compatibility shims (documented, but not idiomatic v4):
```css
@theme {
  --default-ring-width: 3px;
  --default-ring-color: var(--color-blue-500);
}
@layer base {
  *, ::after, ::before, ::backdrop, ::file-selector-button {
    border-color: var(--color-gray-200, currentColor);
  }
}
```

## Verify
In DevTools the focused element shows `box-shadow` with a 3px spread in blue, and bordered elements compute `border-color: oklch(...)` of gray-200 rather than the text color.

## Sources
- https://tailwindcss.com/docs/upgrade-guide#default-ring-width-and-color
- https://tailwindcss.com/docs/upgrade-guide#default-border-color

## Symptom
After moving to Tailwind v4, custom theme values from `tailwind.config.js` (e.g. `bg-brand-500`, `font-display`, extra breakpoints) generate no CSS, or `@apply` of them errors with `Cannot apply unknown utility class`. The config file is still on disk but nothing reads it. Agents often 'fix' this by editing `theme.extend` further, which has no effect.

## Cause
Tailwind CSS v4.0 (2025-01-22) is configured CSS-first. JavaScript config files are still supported for backward compatibility but are no longer detected automatically. The `corePlugins`, `safelist` and `separator` options are not supported at all in v4.

## Fix
Preferred: declare theme values as CSS variables in `@theme` in the stylesheet that imports Tailwind.

Before (`tailwind.config.js`):
```js
module.exports = {
  theme: { extend: {
    colors: { avocado: { 500: 'oklch(0.84 0.18 117.33)' } },
    fontFamily: { display: ['Satoshi', 'sans-serif'] },
    screens: { '3xl': '1920px' },
  } },
}
```
After (`app.css`):
```css
@import "tailwindcss";

@theme {
  --color-avocado-500: oklch(0.84 0.18 117.33);
  --font-display: "Satoshi", "sans-serif";
  --breakpoint-3xl: 1920px;
}
```
Stopgap: keep the JS file and load it explicitly from CSS:
```css
@import "tailwindcss";
@config "../../tailwind.config.js";
```
Plugins move to CSS too: `@plugin "@tailwindcss/typography";`. The official codemod `npx @tailwindcss/upgrade` (Node 20+) can perform this migration.

## Verify
`bg-avocado-500` / `font-display` / `3xl:` produce CSS; in DevTools `:root` exposes `--color-avocado-500`.

